ing budapest a spy hub  there's an increasing number of chinese  students flowing in  to the country and china is known for  using  its citizens forcing them to feed  information  to chinese intelligence and of course  china is mostly not interested  in what's happening in hungary they are  not interested in hungarian high tech  because  there's there are none one of the most  controversial chinese projects in  hungary  is yet to be built by 2024 shanghai's  fudan university wants to open its very  first european  campus in budapest at this site hungary  plans to finance the mega project  reportedly through a 1.5 billion euro  loan  from a chinese bank construction will be  carried out  using chinese materials and labor so  who benefits hungary i think  has vetoed the the most  in favor of china so china is the number  one beneficiary of hungary's disruptive  power inside the eu  but we don't actually see chinese  foreign direct investment  flowing and pouring in to the country  some say that in order to to gain the  trust of the chinese  you have to develop a relationship  on the long run so it means that you  need to put a lot of effort and years  and years  to convince them that you're a trusted  partner  not all hungarians are happy with the  government's close  ties with china and they are taking  action a couple thousand protesters  gathered at budapest kosciutzka recently  to stop the planned construction of food  and university  and to demand affordable housing for  hungarian students  instead at the planned construction site  of the campus  budapest liberal mayor korachon has put  up new street signs  he is a key critic of orban the science  now read free hong kong road  or dalai lama road an attempt to  underline  that possible political and economy gain  should never outweigh speaking up  against human rights violations  so what is the purpose of the vetoes  exercised by orban's government  that hungary only vetoes the decisions  that they feel  that are 